Id: lin043i
Severity: INFO
Category: kernel
Check: check_sysctl
Fix: Set kernel.kptr_restrict=2 in /etc/sysctl.d where root does not need perf or bpftrace symbols.
References: Linux kernel sysctl documentation (admin-guide/sysctl/kernel, kptr_restrict); sysctl.d(5)
Controls: CIS 4.1, 10.5; NIST CM-6, SI-16; ISO 8.9; CE Secure configuration

kernel.kptr_restrict is 1: kernel pointers printed with %pK (in
/proc/kallsyms, /proc/modules and the like) read as zeros to users
without CAP_SYSLOG, and as their true values to root. 2 zeroes them
for everyone, root included, so that a root-level process that is
itself compromised learns nothing about the kernel's layout. Ubuntu
ships 1 (procps' 10-kernel-hardening.conf); Debian, Fedora, Arch and
Alpine leave the kernel's 0 (lin020w). The cost of 2 falls on root's
own tools: perf, bpftrace and crash analysis lose their symbols. A
hardening opportunity, not a weakness: Lynis asks for 2, the CIS
Benchmarks do not name the key.
  # sysctl -w kernel.kptr_restrict=2
